WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.979
Network failures are normal, so they should be tested directly.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:11.143
A secure socket component should have coverage for disconnects, partial reads, partial writes, malformed messages, oversized messages, timeouts,

3
00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:14.724
authentication failures, TLS validation failures, shutdown, and retry behavior.

4
00:00:14.724 --> 00:00:23.479
These tests do not need to model every possible network condition, but they should prove that common failure paths preserve safe state.

5
00:00:23.479 --> 00:00:26.265
Logging is part of the safety story.

6
00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:34.224
Operators need enough information to understand whether a connection failed because of policy, invalid input, timeout, resource pressure, or a

7
00:00:34.224 --> 00:00:35.019
dependency problem.

8
00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:42.581
At the same time, logs should not print secrets, tokens, credentials, private payloads, session keys, or unnecessary internal details.

9
00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:49.346
A helpful log explains the event category and context without turning sensitive network data into stored data.

10
00:00:49.346 --> 00:00:51.335
Error responses need similar balance.

11
00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:58.896
Legitimate callers should receive clear enough responses to correct valid mistakes, but the service should avoid excessive implementation detail.

12
00:00:58.896 --> 00:01:06.457
Internal exception messages, stack traces, parser internals, or raw payload excerpts usually belong in protected diagnostics, not in protocol

13
00:01:06.457 --> 00:01:06.855
responses.

14
00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:11.233
Consistent error mapping also makes clients easier to write and monitor.

15
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.212
Code review should treat socket code as security-sensitive engineering.

16
00:01:15.212 --> 00:01:22.375
Reviewers should check protocol boundaries, resource limits, TLS assumptions, bind behavior, cleanup behavior, and whether failure paths release

17
00:01:22.375 --> 00:01:22.773
resources.

18
00:01:22.773 --> 00:01:27.947
Release review should confirm that tests cover the intended platform and deployment model.

19
00:01:27.947 --> 00:01:33.120
The result is code that is easier to operate and safer to change.
